  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{2x+3}-4\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{2y+3}-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{2y+3}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y+3} & =x+4 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{2y+3})^{3} & =(x+4)^{3} \\\\ 2y+3 & =(x+4)^{3} \\\\ 2y & =(x+4)^{3}-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$",
